---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Playing Through Hate - Replenish Guide | Premodern MTG"
    image="/src/assets/banners/mage.webp"
    description="Winning post-sideboard games is about playing around their hate, not answering all of it. You rarely need a specific answer to every sideboard card—Games 2 and 3 come down to changing your sequencing, timing your stack triggers, and staying disciplined."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Playing Through Hate"
        description="Winning post-sideboard games is about playing around their hate, not answering all of it. You rarely need a specific answer to every sideboard card—Games 2 and 3 come down to changing your sequencing, timing your stack triggers, and staying disciplined."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/mage.webp"
        imageAlt="Meddling Mage"
        imageCaption="Meddling Mage by Christopher Moeller"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Graveyard Interference" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            Because <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                >Replenish</a
            > leans on the graveyard to cheat out big mana costs, opponents will
            bring graveyard hate in post-board games. Beating it comes down to one
            rule: never dump your key cards into the yard without a win already on
            the stack or in hand.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Withered Wretch"
            image="creatures/withered_wretch.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>(Black Aggro, The Rock):</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Withered Wretch"
                    >Withered Wretch</a
                > is dangerous because it exiles from your graveyard over and over,
                at instant speed, and it's stapled to an aggressive body that's also
                racing your life total.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Against black midrange or aggro, save
                your <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                > for Withered Wretch. Kill it before you start firing card-selection
                spells or putting enchantments in your graveyard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Universal Exile:</strong> The simplest answer to an early
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > is just blowing it up with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                > before you go for the combo.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The Stack Trick:</strong> If a Crypt is already in play, don't
                just dump your whole hand into the graveyard. Keep an <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > on the table and hold your combo pieces in hand. Build a graveyard
                that's tempting but not essential—just enough to bait them—then cast
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                >. When they crack Crypt in response to empty your yard, Replenish
                is still on the stack. In response to your own Replenish, activate
                Attunement to pitch a fresh handful of combo enchantments into the
                now-empty graveyard. Replenish resolves right after and brings back
                everything you just discarded.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Phyrexian Furnace"
            image="artifacts/phyrexian_furnace.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Incremental Attrition:</strong> Unlike <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                > only exiles one card at a time—either by sacrificing it or with its
                tap ability.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Don't feed your combo enchantments into
                the graveyard slowly over several turns—that just lets them pick off
                your lock pieces one at a time. Load up in a single turn with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition"
                    >Intuition</a
                > right before you cast <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >. They can only exile one card in response, so the rest of your
                pieces come back and end the game.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Haunting Echoes"
            image="sorceries/haunting_echoes.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Graveyard & Library Extraction:</strong> The scariest hate
                card out of black control decks and Psychatog. A resolved five-mana
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Haunting Echoes">Haunting Echoes</a
                > wipes your whole graveyard and then pulls every copy of those cards
                out of your library too, which basically shuts the combo off.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Since it costs five and it's a sorcery,
                they have to tap way down on their own turn to cast it. Hold your cheap
                counters (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                    >Counterspell</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                    >Arcane Denial</a
                >) for when they hit five mana, or name it with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > in Games 2 and 3. Don't let your graveyard pile up against five-mana
                black control unless you can cast Replenish off it on your next turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Planar Void" image="enchantments/planar_void.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Continuous Exile Constraint:</strong> A one-mana enchantment
                out of Suicide Black and mono-black aggro that exiles every card the
                moment it would hit a graveyard, so nothing ever sticks around for you
                to use.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay & Trigger Manipulation:</strong> Since <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Planar Void">Planar Void</a
                > is an enchantment, your maindeck <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or an instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > kills it cleanly before you go off.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sweepers & Permanent Wipes" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            When opponents can't attack your graveyard, they'll often load up on
            enchantment removal instead, trying to break up your engines once they
            land.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tranquil Domain / Naturalize"
            image="instants/tranquil_domain.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Instant-Speed Destruction:</strong> The classic green answers—they
                destroy your lock pieces as you cast them, or pick apart your board at
                instant speed during combat or your end step.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong>
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > is your best defense here: bring it back alongside the combo and they
                have to spend three spells just to punch through the counters it hands
                out. You can also use <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > under an active Opal-Wave engine—target your own vulnerable enchantments
                in response to their removal, and once the stack clears and Wave's fading
                counters bring them back, your board is safe. And in the sideboard games,
                switching to <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > just turns their naturalize effects into dead cards, since you're now
                winning with a creature instead of enchantments.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Scour" image="instants/scour.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Surgical Eradication:</strong> Mostly a blue-white control
                sideboard card. <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Scour">Scour</a
                > destroys an enchantment on the battlefield and then exiles every copy
                of it from your hand, library, and graveyard. One Scour on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > can take your win condition off the table for the rest of the match.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Don't drop a naked engine enchantment
                into open four-mana white post-board unless you have a counter or a
                plan. The key trick: keep fading counters on your Parallax Wave during
                an Opal-Wave loop, so you can exile your own enchantments in response
                to a Scour targeting them. If the target's already gone when Scour tries
                to resolve, it fizzles and exiles nothing.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Anarchy" image="sorceries/anarchy.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Red Sweeper & Color Hate:</strong> Red aggro decks (Sligh,
                RG Ponza, Goblins) bring in <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                > to wipe your white stuff like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                    >Exalted Angel</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                    >Meddling Mage</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > all in one shot.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Anarchy is a four-mana sorcery, and it
                destroys rather than exiles. First, slow their mana down with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                > to keep them off four mana, or just name it with
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                    >Meddling Mage</a
                >. Second, remember that everything it destroys goes straight to your
                graveyard—so if you're holding a backup <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >, you bring the whole board right back next turn and turn the blowout
                into a game-ending swing.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Reverent Silence"
            image="sorceries/reverent_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Free Enchantment Sweeper:</strong> Elves and green aggro-midrange
                use this as free interaction—they can cast it for zero mana by giving
                you 6 life. Sweeping your whole enchantment board for free can blow out
                early pieces like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement"
                    >Attunement</a
                >, but if you plan around it, the exchange is actually great for you.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> It's a sorcery, so they can only cast
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Reverent Silence">Reverent Silence</a
                > on their own main phase. Early on, take the 6 life happily—every
                enchantment it destroys goes to your graveyard and sets up <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > for free. And once your Opal-Wave engine is online, just respond by
                activating <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > to blink your own board, so it all comes back safely after Reverent
                Silence resolves.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Ray of Revelation / Sylvan Reclamation">
            <p>
                <strong>Flashback & Exile Spot Removal:</strong> Green-white decks
                use <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ray of Revelation">Ray of Revelation</a
                > for a 2-for-1 thanks to Flashback, and you'll also see exile-based
                sweepers like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sylvan Reclamation">Sylvan Reclamation</a
                > that shut off graveyard recursion entirely. Note that countering the
                first cast of Ray doesn't help much—they can just pay one white mana
                to flash it back from the graveyard and destroy your key piece anyway.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Against recurring flashback removal, don't
                lay your enchantments out one at a time. Put them all down at once with
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >, where an instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > can protect them, or just pivot to non-enchantment threats like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                    >Meddling Mage</a
                > that leave their enchantment removal stranded in hand.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Calming Verse">
            <p>
                <strong>Asymmetric Mass Sweeper:</strong> A four-mana green sorcery
                that destroys every enchantment you control while leaving the opponent's
                own permanents untouched.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> You have to be disciplined with your
                counters here: hold <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                    >Counterspell</a
                >, or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                    >Arcane Denial</a
                > for when they hit four mana to cast it. Otherwise, use proactive pieces
                like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Orim's Chant"
                    >Orim's Chant</a
                >, or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                > to stop them from wiping the board on the turn that matters.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Taxation & Cost Increases" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            These permanents sit on the board and try to choke your mana or stop
            your engine loops from working.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Presence of the Master"
            image="enchantments/presence_of_the_master.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>The Replenish Bypass:</strong> It looks terrifying against
                an enchantment deck, but <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Presence of the Master"
                    >Presence of the Master</a
                > only counters enchantment spells you <em>cast</em> from hand. <strong
                    >It does nothing to enchantments coming in off <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    >!</strong
                >
            </p>
            <p>
                Still, once it's in play you can't hardcast combo pieces or answers
                like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                >. Play around it by building your graveyard and winning straight off
                a single Replenish, or lean on instant-speed removal like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > and non-enchantment threats like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Aura of Silence"
            image="enchantments/aura_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Tax & Threat Combination:</strong> You'll see this out of
                White Weenie, BW Control, and the Enchantress mirror. <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                > taxes your artifacts and enchantments by two mana, which makes early
                ramp and hardcasting your engines painfully slow. On top of that, it
                can sacrifice itself to destroy whatever key enchantment you finally
                do resolve.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> This is exactly why the sideboard splits
                between <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                >—Disenchant is a clean two-mana instant that Aura's tax doesn't touch.
                In grindy games where Aura lands early, spend your mana on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to fill the graveyard, then cast a <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > that brings back several enchantments at once; one sacrifice trigger
                can't break up a whole Opal-Wave-Decree lock hitting the board together.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Gloom" image="matchups/moneyball/gloom.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Asymmetric Color Tax:</strong> Black aggro, Suicide Black,
                and The Rock all bring this in. <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                > taxes every white spell and white activated ability by three mana,
                which can badly slow down <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> The good news is Gloom only touches white.
                Your whole blue card-selection package (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition"
                    >Intuition</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Deep Analysis"
                    >Deep Analysis</a
                >) and your counters (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                    >Counterspell</a
                >) work just fine. Against heavily black decks, save your early counters
                for Gloom itself. If it does resolve, use your sol lands (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="City of Traitors">City of Traitors</a
                >) and artifact ramp to just pay through the three-mana tax over a long
                game, or lean on blue tools like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Upheaval"
                    >Upheaval</a
                > and cycling <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Winter Orb" image="artifacts/winter_orb.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Mana Chokepoints:</strong> Common in Stasis, Merfolk, and tempo
                decks. Replenish is built around three- and four-mana spells, so only
                untapping one land a turn really slows down your setup.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Get rid of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Winter Orb">Winter Orb</a
                > right away with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or an instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                >. If it sticks, sol lands like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                > are great because you get two mana off the one land you untap. Best
                of all, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > is a fantastic burst-untapper under Orb: tap all your lands for mana
                first, cast Search, and untap three lands when it resolves—suddenly you've
                got a big pile of mana on an otherwise locked turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Cursed Totem" image="artifacts/cursed_totem.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>The Opalescence Lockout:</strong> A sneaky, easy-to-miss sideboard
                artifact. On its own, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Cursed Totem">Cursed Totem</a
                > doesn't touch your normal enchantments at all. But <strong
                    >the moment <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > enters the battlefield, all of your non-Aura enchantments transform
                    into creatures.</strong
                >
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> With both Opalescence and Cursed Totem
                out, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement"
                    >Attunement</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > are all creatures with activated abilities—so Cursed Totem shuts their
                activations off completely! To avoid this trap, always use <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or an instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > to kill Cursed Totem <em>before</em> Opalescence hits the table.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Dystopia" image="enchantments/dystopia.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Cumulative Color Attrition:</strong> A grinding sideboard
                enchantment out of black control and midrange decks like The Rock.
                Every one of your upkeeps, you have to sacrifice a green or white
                permanent. Left alone, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Dystopia">Dystopia</a
                > chews through your <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> The key points: Dystopia costs the opponent
                more and more life each upkeep, and it only hits green and white—so
                your blue draw-discard engine (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >) and your sol-land base are completely safe. Just build your combo
                up in the graveyard without laying out white permanents early, and when
                you're ready, cast <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > onto a full board. At that point sacrificing one spare white permanent
                to the upkeep trigger doesn't matter, and you swing for the win.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Humility">
            <p>
                <strong>Layered Stat Normalization:</strong> You'll occasionally see
                this as mirror tech or out of UW Control. <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Humility">Humility</a
                > removes all creature abilities and sets every creature to 1/1. Since
                your kill relies on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > turning your non-Aura enchantments into attackers, a Humility that
                resolves after your setup creates messy layer interactions and shuts
                off your creature-based activations.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> When Humility is in play, don't try to
                win with Opalescence creatures or count on creature abilities. Treat
                it as a must-kill target: clear it with an instant-speed
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                > or an existing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > before you turn your graveyard into an attacking board.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Creature Interference" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Precision Bottlenecks:</strong> Opponents fielding blue-white
                builds (UW Landstill, mirrors) frequently introduce <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > post-board to lock down your namesake <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > or foundational combo engines like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Maintain vigilant situational awareness
                regarding which opponent archetypes have access to white and blue
                mana after Games 1. When anticipating opposing Mages, deliberately
                retain spot removal suite representation (leaving in 1–2 copies of
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                > or relying on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >). A single Meddling Mage represents a temporary hurdle rather
                than a hard lockout if you construct your sideboard transitions
                to expect it.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
